      author: "Dr. Karthik Rao", reviewedBy: "Medically reviewed by Dr. Karthik Rao, MDS (Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics)",
      excerpt: "Saving a tooth and removing it both have a place. Here is what decides it, and what each costs over time.",
      body: "When a tooth's nerve is infected, there are two ways to stop the pain: clean and seal the canals (root canal treatment), or remove the tooth.\n\nA root canal keeps your own tooth, your bite and the bone around it. It suits a tooth with enough healthy structure left to hold a crown and gums that are firm around it. It usually takes one or two visits, plus a crown on back teeth.\n\nExtraction is quicker and costs less on the day. It suits a tooth that is cracked down the root, very loose from gum disease, or too broken to rebuild. The gap then needs thought: teeth beside it can tilt, and replacing it later with a bridge or an implant costs more than the root canal would have.\n\nAsk your dentist to show you the X-ray, and to write down both options with their cost over the next few years, not only today.",
